00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:09.462
And now they are full,
stand alone Cloud products.

00:08:09.462 --> 00:08:11.420
MELANIE WARRICK: So what's
new with App Engine?

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:14.090
STEREN GIANNINI: The world
has changed in 10 years,

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:17.850
and a lot of web
frameworks have appeared.

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:20.270
And originally, App
Engine was a little bit

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:23.030
forcing you to use a
certain programming model.

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:25.560
And all of that has changed now.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:29.200
App Engine is now
what we say idiomatic.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:32.419
So in Cloud Next we
introduced what we called

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:34.669
the second generation runtimes.

00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:37.010
And those generation
runtimes really

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:41.130
allow you to use an idiomatic
developer experience.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:43.799
STEWART REICHLING: So the idea
is, I'm a Python developer,

00:08:43.799 --> 00:08:46.460
I want to be able to write
Python code, not necessarily

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:47.150
App Engine code.

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:48.890
Steren is a Node developer.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:51.410
Steren wants to write
Node code, not necessarily

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:52.496
App Engine code.

00:08:52.496 --> 00:08:54.620
And so as part of that, we
have these new runtimes,

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:56.660
three of them right
now to announce.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:58.220
We have Node 8,
that was actually

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:01.550
previewed at Google I/O.
We have Python 3.7 coming

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:04.539
in the next week or so, PHP 7.2.

00:09:04.539 --> 00:09:06.080
I'm actually not
sure when this airs.

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:07.927
It might be out by the
time this comes out.

00:09:07.927 --> 00:09:10.260
And then we're working on
some other languages, as well.

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:12.468
And the idea behind all of
these, the unifying theme,

00:09:12.468 --> 00:09:14.300
is that they are
idiomatic, which

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:17.060
means that you write the
language that you know.

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:19.482
You don't think about
using proprietary stuff.

00:09:19.482 --> 00:09:21.940
MARK MANDEL: And that actually
is probably a good question,

00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:22.439
as well.

00:09:22.439 --> 00:09:23.460
So it's idiomatic.

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:25.010
What am I writing
this stuff for?

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:26.210
Is it, like, websites?

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:28.184
Like, what am I doing
with this stuff?

00:09:28.184 --> 00:09:29.600
STEWART REICHLING:
For sure, yeah.

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:32.270
We see all kinds of
different use cases.

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:35.750
A lot of them are web
apps, microservices,

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:39.420
anything that needs scale,
either scaling up very fast,

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:41.300
so you get a ton of traffic.

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:44.480
App Engine scales automatically
for you to meet that need.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:46.220
Or you're not using
it very frequently.

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:47.900
It scales down to zero.

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:50.180
And so in that case, you're
not paying for anything.

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:52.130
During Oren's talk, there was--

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:54.350
"The New York Times,"
actually, is a great example

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:57.212
where they have these crosswords
that are super popular.

00:09:57.212 --> 00:09:58.670
Right when they
come out, everybody

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:00.400
rushes online to
try and solve them.

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:02.630
Well, how do you provision
infrastructure for that?

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:05.030
You either don't provision
enough, in which case

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:07.119
you're losing customers,
or you way overprovision,

00:10:07.119 --> 00:10:08.660
in which case you're
paying for stuff

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:10.222
that's not even getting used.

00:10:10.222 --> 00:10:11.930
And so App Engine is
the perfect use case

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:13.064
for something like that.

00:10:13.064 --> 00:10:14.480
Suddenly you get
a lot of traffic.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:15.710
It scales up.

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:19.700
STEREN GIANNINI: I'm always
amazed by the creativity

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:20.480
of our customers.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:23.600
Like, so many people build
their entire businesses

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:25.190
on top of App Engine.

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:30.920
Many use cases are about
mobile backends or REST APIs,

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:33.254
or even internet
applications because you just

00:10:33.254 --> 00:10:35.670
deploy your app and then you
don't have to worry about it.

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:36.500
It just runs.

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:40.190
So that abstracts away
the operations for you

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:41.524
to focus on your business value.

00:10:41.524 --> 00:10:43.064
STEWART REICHLING:
And so you've also

00:10:43.064 --> 00:10:45.410
been a big push on getting
headless Chrome and features

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:45.980
like that built in.

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:47.630
Why does that matter
for App Engine?

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:49.040
STEREN GIANNINI: Yeah.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:52.310
So you can easily build
very small services

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:54.920
that are super dedicated
to doing one thing.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:57.260
For example, we put
all of the OS packages

00:10:57.260 --> 00:11:00.470
needed to run headless
Chrome on App Engine.

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:03.560
So if you want to run one
service that just takes

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:06.219
a screenshot of a web
page or generates a PDF,

00:11:06.219 --> 00:11:07.760
you can do that on
App Engine and you

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.300
don't have to worry about it.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:10.910
It just works.

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:11.390
STEWART REICHLING:
So it meets needs--

00:11:11.390 --> 00:11:11.570
MARK MANDEL: That's cool.

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:13.910
STEWART REICHLING: --all the
way from big web apps down

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:15.960
to developer tools,
internal tools

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:17.210
that you're building yourself.

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:18.050
MELANIE WARRICK: What
are some examples

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:18.920
that you've seen
that you've been

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:21.169
pretty impressed by in terms
of how it's been applied?

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:25.500
STEREN GIANNINI: Well, we as--

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:27.390
we said-- we have some
amazing customers.

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:30.744
Like I think the most famous
one is probably Snapchat.

00:11:30.744 --> 00:11:32.160
So that's pretty
amazing what they

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:34.650
built on top of
App Engine, going

00:11:34.650 --> 00:11:38.720
from a very small company
to this massive scale using

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:39.690
our platform.

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:41.850
MARK MANDEL: One of my
favorites, episode 26-- no,

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:42.690
23--

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:44.670
with the-- if anyone
plays video games

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:47.420
like I do-- a Humble Bundle
that runs on top of App Engine.

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:47.650
STEWART REICHLING: OK.

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:47.880
Oh, wow.

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:48.870
MARK MANDEL: And they
actually specifically--

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:50.760
because they do an
event-- well, they

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:52.720
used to do especially
at an event-based model

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:54.540
where it was like, they have
these events for cheap games.

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:56.040
And so they get massive spikes.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:58.500
And having App Engine, they
said it was really great.

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:00.166
STEWART REICHLING:
Yeah, Humble Bundle's

00:12:00.166 --> 00:12:01.430
a great use case for that.

00:12:01.430 --> 00:12:04.494
STEREN GIANNINI: Khan Academy,
we are very proud to host them.

00:12:04.494 --> 00:12:06.660
STEWART REICHLING: "New
York Times," as I mentioned.

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:07.743
You can go on the website.

00:12:07.743 --> 00:12:10.080
There's customers of all
sizes use App Engine,

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:12.070
have been using App
Engine for 10 years.

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:14.770
One of the things that
I hear from customers,

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:17.340
which is pretty cool, is you
often get people who say,

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:19.320
you know, the thing I
launched five years ago,

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:21.070
I can still go use that today.

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:22.240
It still exists today.

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:23.940
It still runs in
its current form.

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:26.320
They haven't been paying
for it that whole time.

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.590
But you see even small things
like my first experience

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:31.470
with App Engine was writing
a portfolio website.

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:34.500
STEREN GIANNINI: I probably
have my own website I deployed.

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:38.079
It was a game website
in 2011, I think.

00:12:38.079 --> 00:12:39.120
It's still running today.

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:41.112
Just hit the URL and it's there.

00:12:41.112 --> 00:12:42.570
MARK MANDEL: That's
pretty amazing.

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:44.250
So Steren, you said something
earlier, which I thought

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:45.030
was interesting,
when we were talking

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:47.190
about the history of
App Engine and how

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:48.590
Datastore was once integrated.

00:12:48.590 --> 00:12:50.610
And I know now Datastore
has been pulled out

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:51.925
as a whole separate product.

00:12:51.925 --> 00:12:54.300
Are there other products that
are upcoming that are maybe

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:55.980
being pulled out of
App Engine so that they

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:57.230
can be used in other contexts?

00:12:57.230 --> 00:13:00.420
STEREN GIANNINI: Yeah, we are
graduating some App Engine

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:04.140
exclusive features into
cloud-wide products.

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:06.350
So Datastore is a
good example of that.

00:13:06.350 --> 00:13:09.930
At Next yesterday we
announced that the task queue

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:13.610
feature of App Engine is now
a standalone Cloud product.

00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:15.300
It's called Cloud Tasks.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:17.850
So task queues are very
useful when you want

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:21.130
to do things asynchronously.

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:24.720
So for example, you have
to do some computation.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:26.280
It can take some
time and you don't

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:28.400
want to delay the
response to your users.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:30.770
You will create a
task in a queue.

00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:32.940
Cloud Task will make
sure that this task

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:36.462
is executed at least once, and
most of the time exactly once.

00:13:36.462 --> 00:13:37.920
STEWART REICHLING:
Cloud Scheduler,

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:39.180
another great example.

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:42.810
App Engine has long had this
built-in cron functionality.

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:44.820
And again, this
stuff, it originated

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:48.660
when these services and concepts
didn't really exist yet.

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:51.420
So there's a service that
you could use previously

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:55.080
to just say, hey, I want
this to run on this schedule.

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:59.250
Now we're exposing that as a
first party cloud services.

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:03.540
So you can make sure that any
arbitrary HTTP target gets

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:06.520
accessed on a certain schedule
by the minute, by the hour,

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:07.850
by the day, fully customizable.

00:14:07.850 --> 00:14:10.350
And these are features that you
can now use with App Engine.

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:11.850
You can use them
with Compute Engine.

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:13.110
You can use them with anything.

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:14.190
MARK MANDEL: That's awesome.

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:16.106
I'm not saying I've just
created an App Engine

00:14:16.106 --> 00:14:18.744
app just to fire cron tasks
off before, but maybe I have.

00:14:18.744 --> 00:14:20.160
STEWART REICHLING:
Yeah, well, you

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:21.980
won't have to do
that in the future.

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:24.480
And we're going to have a bunch
more announcements like this

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:25.897
in the next couple
of months where

00:14:25.897 --> 00:14:27.479
we're taking these
services and making

00:14:27.479 --> 00:14:28.680
them available to everybody.

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:30.770
MELANIE WARRICK: Any that
you can share right now?

00:14:30.770 --> 00:14:32.400
STEWART REICHLING: No comment.

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:33.310
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's a good answer.

00:14:33.310 --> 00:14:34.480
MARK MANDEL: We had to try.

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:36.180
STEREN GIANNINI: We can
talk about the recent things

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:38.429
we launched on the App Engine
to show that we actually

00:14:38.429 --> 00:14:41.580
are heavily investing
into the platform.

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:43.860
So earlier this
year, we completely

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:48.510
changed the autoscaling
algorithm of all of App Engine.

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:51.550
Nobody noticed it, but
it was a massive change

00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:52.990
of our infrastructure.

00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:55.260
And as a consequence,
most of our customers

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:57.930
got better performances
and lower cost.

00:14:57.930 --> 00:15:00.190
So we reduced the
bills of our customers

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:02.240
without them having
to do anything.

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:03.030
STEWART REICHLING: It's
one of the benefits

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:05.220
of having a managed platform
is things like that,

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:06.624
things like security.

00:15:06.624 --> 00:15:08.790
These are things that you
don't have to think about,

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:10.710
which normally you would
have people dedicated

00:15:10.710 --> 00:15:11.910
to think about these
things and you'd

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:14.451
spend a lot of time trying to
figure out how you can optimize

00:15:14.451 --> 00:15:15.344
your infrastructure.

00:15:15.344 --> 00:15:17.010
Because you're on App
Engine, that stuff

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:18.780
is just kind of handled for you.

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:20.571
STEREN GIANNINI: And
another feature that I

00:15:20.571 --> 00:15:22.340
personally like a lot is that--

00:15:22.340 --> 00:15:23.830
so App Engine, you
deploy your app.

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:24.330
Good.

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:26.621
But you probably want to put
that app under your custom

00:15:26.621 --> 00:15:27.540
domain.

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:30.594
And we provide a one-click
HTTPS for your custom domain.

00:15:30.594 --> 00:15:32.760
MARK MANDEL: Security people
must love you for that.

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:33.640
STEREN GIANNINI: Yes.

00:15:33.640 --> 00:15:35.550
MELANIE WARRICK: I was
going to say, yeah.

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:36.900
STEWART REICHLING: I was just
going to talk a little bit more

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:38.730
about what it means
to be idiomatic.

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:42.750
For us, one of the
longstanding pieces of feedback

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:44.250
that we've gotten
from customers is

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:46.920
that, as I mentioned
before, people look at it

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:49.110
and they go, oh, I have to
do these special things.

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:51.450
My app will only work in
this App Engine environment

00:15:51.450 --> 00:15:52.740
if I design it for this.

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:55.149
I maybe already have a
workload that I want to move in

00:15:55.149 --> 00:15:56.940
or I have something
that I want to move out

00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:59.220
to see how it might do in
a different environment.

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:02.070
Because we're launching
these much more open source

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:06.030
experiences, that means that
you can use any library,

00:16:06.030 --> 00:16:07.660
framework, dependency
that you want.

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:11.037
You don't have to use these
proprietary APIs anymore.

00:16:11.037 --> 00:16:12.870
All of these things
should just work the way

00:16:12.870 --> 00:16:13.800
you expect them to work.

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:14.880
STEREN GIANNINI: So
I'm a Node developer.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:16.285
I can get anything
from any npm--

00:16:16.285 --> 00:16:17.160
MARK MANDEL: Oh, wow.

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:18.951
STEREN GIANNINI: --even
the binary modules.

00:16:18.951 --> 00:16:20.310
They just work on App Engine.

00:16:20.310 --> 00:16:22.020
STEWART REICHLING: Same for
Python. requirements.txt,

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:23.311
put your dependencies in there.

00:16:23.311 --> 00:16:25.310
When you deploy, it all
gets installed for you

00:16:25.310 --> 00:16:27.380
with the correct versions
that you've requested.

00:16:27.380 --> 00:16:29.005
We're even looking
into other standards

00:16:29.005 --> 00:16:31.037
like PIP file because
we love Python.

00:16:31.037 --> 00:16:33.620
We want to make sure this is the
best place to run your Python

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:34.740
applications.

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:36.115
STEREN GIANNINI:
And we also made

00:16:36.115 --> 00:16:39.852
sure to put some very useful
OS packages in your base image,

00:16:39.852 --> 00:16:41.060
which is, by the way, Ubuntu.

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:43.220
So you get a regular
Ubuntu filled

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:46.310
with some pretty useful
packages, like ImageMagick

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:49.304
or FFmpeg and everything
to run headless Chrome,

00:16:49.304 --> 00:16:49.970
as we mentioned.

00:16:49.970 --> 00:16:52.511
MARK MANDEL: You seem very, very
happy about headless Chrome.

00:16:52.511 --> 00:16:53.470
STEREN GIANNINI: Yeah.

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:54.970
It's a nice use case, right?

00:16:54.970 --> 00:16:56.267
Just run Chrome on your server.

00:16:56.267 --> 00:16:57.350
MARK MANDEL: That's great.

00:16:57.350 --> 00:17:00.354
So are you able to talk about
why this change happened?

00:17:00.354 --> 00:17:01.770
Like, it seems
like there's like--

00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:04.270
I think you used the term second
generation earlier, Steren?

00:17:04.270 --> 00:17:06.380
STEWART REICHLING: Yeah,
I think a big part of it

00:17:06.380 --> 00:17:07.680
is customer feedback.

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:09.500
We've heard from
customers for a long time

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:11.970
that having to think
about your application

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:14.532
in this specific model
is an impediment.

00:17:14.532 --> 00:17:16.490
It's also part of the
reason why some customers

00:17:16.490 --> 00:17:19.227
choose things like VMs
instead, because it gives them

00:17:19.227 --> 00:17:20.810
that fully-baked
experience where they

00:17:20.810 --> 00:17:22.366
can install whatever they want.

00:17:22.366 --> 00:17:23.990
So lifting these
restrictions is really

00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:25.490
based on customer feedback.

00:17:25.490 --> 00:17:27.156
STEREN GIANNINI: And
to achieve that, we

00:17:27.156 --> 00:17:29.090
leveraged a very big
investment that we've

00:17:29.090 --> 00:17:31.400
made into our infrastructure.

00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:34.070
So technically, those
second generation runtimes

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:36.770
are enabled by a
new technical stack.

00:17:36.770 --> 00:17:40.160
It's all based on gvisor,
which is a container runtime

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:40.944
sandbox--

00:17:40.944 --> 00:17:42.360
which is, by the
way, open source.

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:44.120
You can check it out on GitHub.

00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:47.330
So all of those runtimes, new
runtimes, second generation

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:50.900
runtimes, are built using
that new sandbox, which

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:55.070
allows us to really follow
this idiomatic experience,

00:17:55.070 --> 00:17:58.220
bringing any module you
want in that sandbox.

00:17:58.220 --> 00:17:59.870
STEWART REICHLING:
And just rewinding,

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:02.750
when App Engine first came into
existence, a lot of this stuff

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:04.470
did not even exist.

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:07.340
And so now we have
this new sandbox.

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:09.440
Previously, you had to
do a whole lot of things

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:12.410
to the language itself in
order to make it run securely

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:13.280
on our data centers.

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:14.000
STEREN GIANNINI:
Yeah, we had to.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:15.375
MELANIE WARRICK:
Just you, right?

00:18:15.375 --> 00:18:15.961
Just you.

00:18:15.961 --> 00:18:17.210
MARK MANDEL: It's just Steren.

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:18.530
I was going to say,
does that mean, then,

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:20.630
that I know some feedback
I've heard previously,

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:22.400
like, sometimes we
aren't necessarily as

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:24.210
fast as our users would
like, I would say,

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:26.960
in updating versions of
languages and stuff like that.

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:28.160
Is this going to mean
that we're going to get

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:29.410
those updates a lot faster?

00:18:29.410 --> 00:18:29.900
STEREN GIANNINI: So exactly.

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:31.670
This new technical
stack will allow

00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:34.780
us to first deliver new
languages to App Engine

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:35.990
standard environment.

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:39.010
So NodeJS is the first example
of a new language which

00:18:39.010 --> 00:18:41.810
we introduced on the
standard environment,

00:18:41.810 --> 00:18:45.290
and also will allow us to
first update for other existing

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:48.437
languages and keep updating
them as the releases comes in.

00:18:48.437 --> 00:18:51.020
STEWART REICHLING: Yeah, and as
I've mentioned multiple times,

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:52.811
everything should just
work out of the box.

00:18:52.811 --> 00:18:54.550
If it doesn't, you
know, get in touch.

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:56.050
Put our emails on the website.

00:18:56.050 --> 00:18:57.300
We want to hear your feedback.

00:18:57.300 --> 00:18:59.210
So if something doesn't work the
way you expect, please let us

00:18:59.210 --> 00:18:59.390
know.

00:18:59.390 --> 00:19:00.800
MARK MANDEL: So everything
should work out of the box?

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:01.970
STEWART REICHLING: It
should work out of the box.

00:19:01.970 --> 00:19:02.490
MARK MANDEL: Out of the box.

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:04.280
MELANIE WARRICK:
Or they call you.

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:05.402
MARK MANDEL: We'll put
your email on there.

00:19:05.402 --> 00:19:05.480
It'll be fine.

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:06.680
MELANIE WARRICK: And
your phone number.

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:07.100
STEREN GIANNINI: Please do.

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:07.665
Please do.

00:19:07.665 --> 00:19:09.290
MELANIE WARRICK: So
in terms of working

00:19:09.290 --> 00:19:12.050
with App Engine, what are
resources you would recommend?

00:19:12.050 --> 00:19:13.050
STEWART REICHLING: Sure.

00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:15.170
So I would foremost
recommend our docs pages.

00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:16.670
That's where we
try to put all of

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:19.405
the Getting Started information
you need, everything like that.

00:19:19.405 --> 00:19:21.530
Whereas previously we had
this very heavyweight doc

00:19:21.530 --> 00:19:24.229
set that told you all about
the specific intricacies

00:19:24.229 --> 00:19:26.270
that you needed to know
since these were slightly

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:28.070
different environments,
it's much lighter

00:19:28.070 --> 00:19:30.590
now because most of the stuff
that you actually needed

00:19:30.590 --> 00:19:32.280
to know, you can find anywhere.

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:35.090
It's just if I know how to write
Python, my Python should work.

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:37.366
If I know how to write
Node, my Node should work.

00:19:37.366 --> 00:19:38.990
You don't really need
to learn and keep

00:19:38.990 --> 00:19:40.060
as much in your head anymore.

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:41.060
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:19:41.060 --> 00:19:43.643
STEREN GIANNINI: So for example,
if you're a NodeJS developer,

00:19:43.643 --> 00:19:46.290
you probably have
already an existing app.

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:48.740
Well, without any
modification, there

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:52.010
is a very high probability
that it will just work

00:19:52.010 --> 00:19:53.390
if you deploy it to App Engine.

00:19:53.390 --> 00:19:54.848
So that could be
a good start, too,

00:19:54.848 --> 00:19:56.660
trying to port an
existing app and bring it

00:19:56.660 --> 00:20:00.155
to App Engine with just one
command, gcloud app deploy,

00:20:00.155 --> 00:20:01.030
deploys to the cloud.

00:20:01.030 --> 00:20:02.821
STEWART REICHLING: Let
me also add to that,

00:20:02.821 --> 00:20:04.310
there is a generous free tier.

00:20:04.310 --> 00:20:07.770
You can just deploy your app,
try it out, see how it works.

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:09.260
If it doesn't work, no sweat.

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:11.030
If you want something
different, no sweat.

00:20:11.030 --> 00:20:12.446
But we actually
think you're going

00:20:12.446 --> 00:20:13.620
to like it and stick to it.

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:15.320
MARK MANDEL: So it's an
interesting question, I think--

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:16.861
I think it's an
interesting question.

00:20:16.861 --> 00:20:17.930
That's why I'm asking it.

00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:20.180
For those people who are
currently on App Engine

00:20:20.180 --> 00:20:22.040
and have apps
running, is like, do

00:20:22.040 --> 00:20:24.410
they need to change their
code or is it still fine?

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:25.452
Or what's the deal there?

00:20:25.452 --> 00:20:27.035
STEWART REICHLING:
Before Node 8 there

00:20:27.035 --> 00:20:28.460
were no existing
Node 8 customers.

00:20:28.460 --> 00:20:29.040
MARK MANDEL: That's true.

00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:31.430
STEWART REICHLING: So this
is mostly a Python question.

00:20:31.430 --> 00:20:33.500
There's still the
Python 2.7 environment.

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:35.594
For Python, 3.7,
yes, if you're just

00:20:35.594 --> 00:20:37.010
trying to migrate
your app, you're

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:38.790
going to have to make
some changes to it.

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:41.270
We think those changes
will make your workloads

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:43.550
more portable in the future,
better in the future,

00:20:43.550 --> 00:20:44.897
and more future-proof.

00:20:44.897 --> 00:20:46.730
But as I said, you know,
your existing stuff

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:47.900
will continue to run.

00:20:47.900 --> 00:20:50.480
This new runtime is really
an incremental new feature

00:20:50.480 --> 00:20:52.771
to which you can deploy
now idiomatic apps the way

00:20:52.771 --> 00:20:54.520
you would expect in
any other environment.

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:55.670
MARK MANDEL: So
nothing's breaking.

00:20:55.670 --> 00:20:57.650
It's just if you want to
keep pushing new stuff--

00:20:57.650 --> 00:20:58.280
STEWART REICHLING:
That's exactly right.

00:20:58.280 --> 00:20:59.270
MARK MANDEL: Beautiful.

00:20:59.270 --> 00:21:00.755
So who's using this right now?

00:21:00.755 --> 00:21:02.160
Are people using
the new runtime?

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:04.160
Have you got any people
who are trying this out?

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:06.980
STEREN GIANNINI: So we announced
the availability of NodeJS

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:09.390
on App Engine standard
environment at Google I/O

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:10.700
earlier this year.

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:13.130
So today we've seen some
pretty good adoption

00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:15.050
from the Node community.

00:21:15.050 --> 00:21:17.890
And the new runtimes are
just opening up right now.

00:21:17.890 --> 00:21:20.960
STEWART REICHLING: Yeah, so
Python 3.7, PHP 7.2, those

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:22.520
are still in private
alpha right now.

00:21:22.520 --> 00:21:25.900
We've got customers using them,
giving us feedback every day.

00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:29.590
Expect those to go to beta very
soon, or maybe already in beta.

00:21:29.590 --> 00:21:32.710
STEREN GIANNINI: And by the
way, we are working on Go 1.11.

00:21:32.710 --> 00:21:34.210
MARK MANDEL: Very
good to hear that.

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:36.010
MELANIE WARRICK: So I know we've
