00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:36.490
MARK MANDEL:
Question of the week?

00:24:36.490 --> 00:24:37.323
MELANIE WARRICK: OK.

00:24:37.323 --> 00:24:39.400
So we read in that
article, L1TF,

00:24:39.400 --> 00:24:41.751
about how we as Google are
recommending that people make

00:24:41.751 --> 00:24:43.000
sure they update their images.

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:44.290
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, you
want to update your image.

00:24:44.290 --> 00:24:45.170
What does that mean?

00:24:45.170 --> 00:24:47.530
And I think that boils
down to sort of where

00:24:47.530 --> 00:24:48.880
you're running your code.

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:50.950
I think we should probably just
talk about two particular ones.

00:24:50.950 --> 00:24:53.170
One is, say, if you're
running your code on something

00:24:53.170 --> 00:24:55.139
like a GCE instance, like
on a virtual machine,

00:24:55.139 --> 00:24:57.430
or whether you're running
something inside a container.

00:24:57.430 --> 00:24:59.346
I think those are the
two most important ones.

00:24:59.346 --> 00:25:00.910
If you're running
it on top of GCE,

00:25:00.910 --> 00:25:03.910
it's basically probably either
a Windows or Linux operating

00:25:03.910 --> 00:25:04.910
system.

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:06.534
So if you're running,
say, like Debian,

00:25:06.534 --> 00:25:08.830
which I'm most familiar
with, doing things like apt

00:25:08.830 --> 00:25:11.830
get upgrade or get full
upgrade to do kernel updates,

00:25:11.830 --> 00:25:14.750
as well, is important to do on
a regular basis, regardless.

00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:16.270
And so when you
do that, make sure

00:25:16.270 --> 00:25:18.100
if you're sitting behind a load
balancer, which you probably

00:25:18.100 --> 00:25:19.630
are, then you can
then roll those

00:25:19.630 --> 00:25:21.730
through behind your scenes
so that you don't necessarily

00:25:21.730 --> 00:25:23.080
have to have any downtime
or anything like that,

00:25:23.080 --> 00:25:23.880
which is super nice.

00:25:23.880 --> 00:25:25.588
So make sure those
things are up to date.

00:25:25.588 --> 00:25:27.250
If you're running
inside a container,

00:25:27.250 --> 00:25:28.501
very much the same thing.

00:25:28.501 --> 00:25:30.250
So you may want to
update your base image.

00:25:30.250 --> 00:25:31.630
Make sure you're running
from the latest base

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:32.712
core operating system.

00:25:32.712 --> 00:25:34.420
And if there are
particular packages that

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:36.919
need to be updated because
of certain security things,

00:25:36.919 --> 00:25:38.710
make sure you get on
top of those, as well.

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:40.084
It's worth noting,
as well, we do

00:25:40.084 --> 00:25:42.541
have the automated container
image security scanning,

00:25:42.541 --> 00:25:43.040
as well.

00:25:43.040 --> 00:25:44.950
So make sure you check
that on a regular basis.

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:46.300
That will actually
give you notifications

00:25:46.300 --> 00:25:47.950
to let you know if there
are any particular security

00:25:47.950 --> 00:25:49.962
issues inside your
images, as well, which

00:25:49.962 --> 00:25:51.670
